One common way this occurs is through **Path Traversal** vulnerabilities, where an application takes user input to construct a file path without proper validation. This allows an attacker to access sensitive files on the server (like `/etc/passwd`) by using `../` sequences.

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Paths;

public class FileServer {
    public String readFile(String filename) throws IOException {
        // This method is vulnerable to Path Traversal attacks because it 
        // concatenates raw user input directly into a file path.
        return new String(Files.readAllBytes(Paths.get("/var/www/app/data/" + filename)));
    }
}
```

***

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.nio.file.Paths;

public class FileServer {
    private static final Path BASE_DIRECTORY = Paths.get("/var/www/app/data/").toAbsolutePath().normalize();

    public String readFile(String filename) throws IOException {
        // Normalize the input path to resolve ".." sequences
        Path requestedPath = BASE_DIRECTORY.resolve(filename).normalize();

        // Ensure the resulting path still starts with the intended base directory
        if (!requestedPath.startsWith(BASE_DIRECTORY)) {
            throw new SecurityException("Access Denied: Invalid file path.");


        // Now it is safe to read the file
        return new String(Files.readAllBytes(requestedPath));
    }
}
```

